3. Material that is not ours

   - The quotations of the Tractatus in German and in the Ogden/Ramsey
     translation (1922) are in the public domain in the European Union and
     the United States.
   - Wittgenstein's works first published after his death in 1951, such as
     the Philosophische Untersuchungen (1953), are in the public domain in
     the European Union since 2022. In the United States they remain under
     copyright for 95 years from first publication (the Untersuchungen to
     the end of 2048). Short passages are quoted, in German or in our own
     Greek translation, with credit, for commentary; the CC BY 4.0 licence
     of section 1 does not cover them.
   - The Tractatus page (docs/tractatus.html) quotes the translation of
     D. F. Pears and B. F. McGuinness (Routledge & Kegan Paul, 1961) for 65
     propositions, about 1,200 words in all, next to the German text and
     the Ogden/Ramsey translation, in order to compare the translations.
     The essay quotes proposition 7 in the Greek translation of Thanasis
     Kitsopoulos (Papazisis, 1978). Both translations are still under
     copyright, which remains with their owners. They are quoted with
     credit, for comparison and commentary; the CC BY 4.0 licence of
     section 1 does not cover them.
   - Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus/5740-pdf.pdf is the Project Gutenberg
     eBook #5740 and carries the Project Gutenberg License stated inside it.
   - The tree-view PDF of the Ludwig Wittgenstein Project states that its text
     and translation are in the public domain; see the notice inside it.
   - Klement's side-by-side edition is not included, because it contains the
     Pears/McGuinness translation, which is still under copyright. It is
     available from its editor at http://people.umass.edu/klement/tlp/.
